    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey so im running an organic kale farm out in the pacific northwest and im seeing some yellowing on the lower leaves like a nitrogen deficiency. soil ph is sitting at 6.2 right now and i just put down some compost a few weeks back. should i go with bone meal or blood meal to bump up the nitrogen without messing up my ph too much?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A nitrogen deficiency on lower leaves in kale, especially when you've just composted, is a common issue. While bone meal is a good source of phosphorus and calcium, blood meal will give you a much faster, more direct nitrogen boost. Since your pH is at 6.2, which is generally good for kale, you can likely apply blood meal without causing a significant pH shift. However, remember that blood meal is a quick fix, and you'll want to work on soil health long-term, like a good, balanced compost application.",
      "source": "target",
